Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 06:15:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Essential Eight: validate the linking, strategy by
 strategy
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Tested by hand, one strategy at a time, against what the ISM controls actually say
rather than against what the mapping claims. All eight reach the ISM. Three
defects, and two of them put controls under a strategy they are not.

Sixteen of the thirty-five patch links pointed at the wrong strategy. An alias took
a whole ISM section, and 'Mitigating known vulnerabilities' holds the application
patching controls and the operating system ones together — the ISM tells them apart
in the control text, not in the heading. So Patch Applications reached six
operating-system controls including ISM-1501, "Operating systems that are no longer
supported by vendors are replaced", and Patch OS Systems reached ten about office
suites, drivers, firmware and online services. An alias may now narrow a section by
what the control itself says, and both readings are written where they can be argued
with. Patch Applications 19 controls to 13, Patch OS Systems 16 to 7, both clean in
each direction.

Configure MS Office Macros reached seven controls with no macro in them — OLE
activation, child processes, code injection, trusted publishers. That is Office
hardening, which is User Application Hardening, a different strategy. 17 to 10.

The AESCSF loader stored a maturity level in a tag called essential_eight_strategy.
The workbook's "Essential Eight:" field states the maturity level of the ISM control
being cited and its only values in the entire file are "ML2, ML3", "ML3" and "N/A",
so a practice read as mapping to a strategy called ML3. Renamed to
cited_ism_essential_eight_maturity, and N/A is no longer stored as a value.

Also corrected: three strategy names match an ISM heading word for word, not two.
Multi-factor Authentication does as well as Application Control and User Application
Hardening, and all three are published-tag while the other five are derived.

Eight cases in tests/test_relate.py and three matrix entries, one of them a repoint
of shipped-set-incomplete, which the matrix's own staleness check caught after the
line it patched gained two entries.

